WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.191 --> 00:00:02.191
- I knew when I said goodbye
to him that it wasn't goodbye

00:00:02.191 --> 00:00:04.450
forever and I'd see him again
and when I see him again

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:06.522
he won't be in pain anymore.

00:00:09.572 --> 00:00:11.153
And he won't be weak.

00:00:11.153 --> 00:00:15.733
(slow music)

00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:19.253
- I was six years old
when my mom passed away.

00:00:19.253 --> 00:00:22.253
- My mom died when I was 17, she was sick.

00:00:22.253 --> 00:00:24.893
She had cancer the
majority of my childhood.

00:00:24.893 --> 00:00:27.713
- My dad was sick for as
long as I can remember.

00:00:27.713 --> 00:00:30.813
When I was born he was
just recently diagnosed

00:00:30.813 --> 00:00:32.413
with multiple sclerosis.

00:00:32.413 --> 00:00:34.013
- You know, I went and
talked to my mom and I,

00:00:34.013 --> 00:00:36.553
you know, I sat down next
to her bed and I told her,

00:00:36.553 --> 00:00:38.513
I said, "This is what the
doctors have told us."

00:00:38.513 --> 00:00:40.973
They said there might be a
solution, but we might have to,

00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:44.513
you know, go across the
country to this doctor's place

00:00:44.513 --> 00:00:46.153
to try it out.

00:00:46.153 --> 00:00:48.543
And she just looked at me
and she's like, "No more."

00:00:49.453 --> 00:00:50.903
I can't.

00:00:50.903 --> 00:00:53.673
- My father who was young and didn't smoke

00:00:53.673 --> 00:00:57.573
came down with pancreatic
cancer and within less than

00:00:57.573 --> 00:00:59.393
six months he had passed away.

00:00:59.393 --> 00:01:01.053
- My father died first.

00:01:01.053 --> 00:01:04.293
He had arterial sclerotic
heart disease so he had a

00:01:04.293 --> 00:01:05.913
massive heart attack.

00:01:05.913 --> 00:01:09.653
And then my mother, she
died about five months later

00:01:09.653 --> 00:01:13.513
and just had, like, a sudden
cardiac arrest and I was

00:01:13.513 --> 00:01:15.393
on the phone with her when it
happened, but I didn't know

00:01:15.393 --> 00:01:16.713
that she had died.

00:01:16.713 --> 00:01:19.073
- We always had this thing
in our family when you,

00:01:19.073 --> 00:01:21.013
you know, love you, love you more.

00:01:21.013 --> 00:01:24.313
I believe it was me to him, "Love ya."

00:01:24.313 --> 00:01:26.873
And then on my way out the
door I heard, "Love you more."

00:01:26.873 --> 00:01:29.413
And that was the last
thing he ever said to me.

00:01:29.413 --> 00:01:31.913
- And he was throwing up
due to the chemo therapy.

00:01:31.913 --> 00:01:36.913
I was young and I walked past
it and I recoiled and said

00:01:37.513 --> 00:01:39.293
gross and ran away.

00:01:39.293 --> 00:01:44.293
Ever since then, that's
just replayed in my head

00:01:44.673 --> 00:01:45.813
since childhood.

00:01:45.813 --> 00:01:47.833
- There were so many unknowns happening.

00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:50.953
Like, I felt that it was
my fault, then I realized

00:01:50.953 --> 00:01:52.213
it wasn't my fault.

00:01:52.213 --> 00:01:55.233
- I remember walking
out of the funeral home

00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:59.193
holding the casket and I
grabbed my little brother

00:01:59.193 --> 00:02:01.973
and I hugged him, I said,
"I promise you everything's

00:02:01.973 --> 00:02:04.593
"gonna be okay, I'm gonna make sure of it.

00:02:04.593 --> 00:02:06.973
"Everything's gonna be fine."

00:02:06.973 --> 00:02:09.673
And I hope I did the right thing.

00:02:09.673 --> 00:02:14.033
I'm hoping that I was able
to do what, at least half

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:16.433
my mother was able to do for me.

00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:18.933
- It was mother's day
and everyone was, like,

00:02:18.933 --> 00:02:22.093
crocheting these things for
their mom and I was the only

00:02:22.093 --> 00:02:24.553
kid in class that, like, didn't.

00:02:24.553 --> 00:02:29.553
So then I just did it for my
grandma which sounds weird.

00:02:30.243 --> 00:02:32.263
So, yeah, that's when

00:02:34.312 --> 00:02:36.283
I kind of understood that, like,

00:02:38.313 --> 00:02:40.933
that everyone else kind of
had this thing and I didn't.

00:02:40.933 --> 00:02:44.313
- You never really lose certain
memories or certain parts

00:02:44.313 --> 00:02:47.652
of their personality and I,
to this day, will be watching

00:02:47.652 --> 00:02:50.933
a movie that I watched with
him several times or a TV show

00:02:50.933 --> 00:02:53.753
that I watched several times
with him and I can still hear

00:02:53.753 --> 00:02:55.153
the parts where he laughed.

00:02:55.153 --> 00:02:58.093
- That void is always gonna
be there and it's a part of

00:02:58.093 --> 00:03:00.853
who I am today, but it
doesn't have to pull me in,

00:03:00.853 --> 00:03:03.733
it doesn't have to take over me.

00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:06.233
And it's something that
I work on all the time.

00:03:06.233 --> 00:03:08.113
- I had a therapist for the
first six months after it

00:03:08.113 --> 00:03:10.933
happened 'cause it helps to
talk to someone without bias.

00:03:10.933 --> 00:03:12.473
- I go to therapy.

00:03:12.473 --> 00:03:15.232
Yeah, I go to therapy because
this isn't an issue that's

00:03:15.232 --> 00:03:17.293
just gonna, like, go away.

00:03:17.293 --> 00:03:19.953
You are going to deal with
the loss of your parents

00:03:19.953 --> 00:03:21.093
for years.

00:03:21.093 --> 00:03:22.093
- If there's somebody that you care about,

00:03:22.093 --> 00:03:24.633
you need to let them know.

00:03:24.633 --> 00:03:27.773
And just don't waste time
'cause the biggest things

00:03:27.773 --> 00:03:30.593
you learn because you can
be taken today, tomorrow,

00:03:30.593 --> 00:03:32.473
in 50 years, you don't know.

00:03:32.473 --> 00:03:35.613
- I learned a lot in those
16 years and I, like,

00:03:35.613 --> 00:03:36.873
cherish those memories.

00:03:36.873 --> 00:03:40.653
- I always tell my friends,
you know, cherish your parents.

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:43.253
You never know when they're gonna go.

00:03:43.253 --> 00:03:46.213
- Cherish the time you had
with them and share that

00:03:46.213 --> 00:03:49.353
with people because everyone's
in it together and we all

00:03:49.353 --> 00:03:50.813
are going through the same thing.

00:03:50.813 --> 00:03:54.603
We are in a struggle together
and together we are stronger.

